#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# `bin/seed` — put the development fixture into D1 (REQ-192).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. REQ-190 replaced nine migrations with one baseline and wiped
# the database rather than migrating it, so a fresh clone comes up with a schema
# and no people. Everything that used to be in the local D1 arrived from a
# migration that has since been deleted, a test fixture, and a series of
# `wrangler d1 execute` statements typed into a terminal during CHAT-23 — none of
# it repeatable, none of it written down. `db/dev-seed.sql` is the written-down
# version and this feeds it to wrangler.
#
# LOCAL BY DEFAULT, AND `--remote` HAS TO BE TYPED. That is a safety property
# rather than a convenience. The fixture carries `alice@plumbing.example` and
# `bob@example.com`, and a fixture reaching a real deployment is the same defect
# REQ-190 just took out of migration `0005` — invented people in a database
# customers are in. Defaulting to local means the destructive target is named out
# loud by whoever meant it.
#
# IT DOES NOT SEED AN OPERATOR. `PLATFORM_ADMINS` in `apps/control-app/wrangler.toml`
# (or a `.dev.vars` override) is how somebody gets into an empty deployment, and
# there is exactly one way to write those rows — see `ensurePlatformOperator`.
#
# Usage:
#   ./bin/seed              # the local D1 that `wrangler dev` serves
#   ./bin/seed --remote     # the deployed one. Read the paragraph above first.
#   ./bin/seed --help
set -euo pipefail

here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
repo_root="$(cd "$here/.." && pwd)"
seed_file="$repo_root/db/dev-seed.sql"

target="--local"
for arg in "$@"; do
  case "$arg" in
    --remote) target="--remote" ;;
    --local)  target="--local" ;;
    -h|--help)
      sed -n '2,26p' "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" | sed 's|^# \{0,1\}||'
      exit 0
      ;;
    *)
      echo "seed: unknown argument '$arg' (try --help)" >&2
      exit 2
      ;;
  esac
done

if [ "$target" = "--remote" ]; then
  echo "seed: writing the DEVELOPMENT FIXTURE to the REMOTE D1." >&2
  echo "seed: this puts invented people into the database real customers are in." >&2
fi

# `--file`, not `--command`, so SQLite parses the statements rather than this
# script guessing where they end. The file's prose contains semicolons and
# apostrophes, both of which a shell-side splitter gets wrong.
#
# WRANGLER'S OUTPUT IS HELD BACK UNLESS SOMETHING FAILS. It prints a JSON result
# object per statement — nineteen of them, all empty, because every statement is
# an INSERT — and that scrolls the only part an operator came to read off the
# screen. On a failure the whole thing is emitted, because then it is the answer.
if ! output=$(cd "$repo_root/apps/control-app" \
      && npx wrangler d1 execute DB "$target" --file "$seed_file" 2>&1); then
  echo "$output" >&2
  echo "seed: wrangler refused the seed (see above). Has the schema been applied?" >&2
  echo "seed:   cd apps/control-app && npx wrangler d1 migrations apply DB $target" >&2
  exit 1
fi

cat <<'REPORT'

Seeded (db/dev-seed.sql, idempotent — re-running changes nothing):

  alice@plumbing.example   signed up; owns three businesses
  alice@oldsalon.example   the SAME person, a second and non-primary address
    Alice's Plumbing       an open-ended grant
    Alice's Lettings       an open-ended grant — several businesses, one account
    Alice's Old Salon      a grant that ENDED: present in the switcher, unselectable
  bob@example.com          in Alice's Plumbing — invited, and signed up
  carol@example.com        in Alice's Plumbing — invited, never came
  dave@example.com         in Alice's Plumbing — a lead, never invited

NOT seeded, deliberately:

  the platform operator    PLATFORM_ADMINS writes those rows, and nothing else may
  any site                 these are identity fixtures; `1c push <slug>` adds one

To sign in as any of them, without a Cloudflare account:

  ./bin/access-sim --print-env > .dev.vars.local
  ./bin/access-sim &
  cd apps/control-app && npx wrangler dev --port 8788 \
    --env-file .dev.vars --env-file ../../.dev.vars.local

  then open http://127.0.0.1:8799/login

Only Alice reaches the builder, at either of her addresses — she is one person
with two, which is what REQ-191 moved the address off `users` to make possible.
The other three are people of Alice's business, not of this deployment's, so
`admit` answers `no_user`: Bob's login reaches no app because Alice's Plumbing
does not have one (DOC-42 §1).
REPORT
